# Expectation record for 05_pointers/006_function_pointer_tables.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_function_pointer_tables
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Dispatch through arrays of function pointers, including a two-dimensional table
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every index into a table is inside its bounds and every entry is initialized before it is
called, through a compatible type.  The row span is taken between pointers to rows of the
same array object and combined with the column count, never by subtracting element pointers
belonging to two different rows, which would not be a subtraction within one object.  No
function address is printed.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
